        "Fig. 1. Algorithm for LTBI testing and TB preventive treatment in individuals at risk"
      ],
      "labels": [
        {
          "text": "Asymptomatic infants <1 year with HIV are only treated for LTBI if they are household contacts of TB. TST or IGRA may identify PLHIV who will benefit most from preventive treatment. Chest radiography (CXR) may",
          "bbox": [
            471.65,
            68.65,
            482.15,
            771.02
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "SegoeUI-Light"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            8.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "1. If <10 years, any one of current cough or fever or history of contact with TB or reported weight loss or confirmed weight loss >5% since last visit or growth curve flattening or weight for age <-2 Z-scores.",
          "bbox": [
            461.65,
            68.71,
            472.15,
            771.02
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "SegoeUI-Light"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            8.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "2. Any one of cough or fever or night sweats or haemoptysis or weight loss or chest pain or shortness of breath or fatigue. In children <5 years, they should also be free of anorexia, failure to thrive, not eating well,",
          "bbox": [
            493.92,
            68.76,
            504.42,
            771.02
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "SegoeUI-Light"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            8.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Other risk group3",
          "bbox": [
            95.43,
            141.59,
            109.94,
            230.8
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": true,
          "fonts": [
            "SegoeUI-Bold"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            6.41,
            11.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "4. Including acute or chronic hepatitis; peripheral neuropathy (if isoniazid is used); regular and heavy alcohol consumption. Pregnancy or a previous history of TB are not contraindications.",
